intersection elements (crossing or roundabouts) are present in each scenario, together with three traffic lights. The different paths are extrapolated from a unique larger map containing all of them, differing in the positioning of traffic signals that induce the user to choose a direction instead of another. All of them start precisely with the same highway section, conveying into the urban area. Route information is supplied by signals indicating the direction to Milan, guiding the user to specific roads. Four different distraction (examples in Fig. 6) events have been implemented (see Table 1) to increase the cognitive workload and approach a real-life driving experience. The presence of distractors is one element that distinguishes laboratory and real-life settings (Bock et al. 2019). Therefore, it can increase the ecological validity of an experiment.
Moreover, an audio track consisting of a car engine’s sound was added to the simulation to provide more realism and was provided through the headphones included in the Oculus Rift device.
